## Static Instrumentation Review

`support-affected2.py:13-25` loads the frozen candidate into a separate module,
retains the original `_row_fragments`, and wraps it with a recording function.
The wrapper saves `ink.copy()` before delegating to the original function,
returning its unchanged result. It does not mutate the counting array, invent
projection results or modify a file. The original `_row_fragments` is read-only.
The in-memory wrapper exists only in the saved probe process.

The frozen candidate's first `_row_fragments` call is at line577, before the
component filter; its second is at line653, immediately after that filter.
There are no intervening calls to that function. Thus `snapshots[1]` is exactly
the counting-copy boundary claimed by the probe, not the caller's untouched
input, the separate support copy or a later merged representation.
`snapshots.clear()` at probe line43 prevents cross-case snapshot contamination.
Probe lines47-49 assert component-sensitive retained area and input equality.

## Support Boundary

Both synthetic cases have 40-pixel full glyphs and the same connected sparse
component: rows `[100,116)`, columns `[250,383)`, area266, density0.125.
Its height16 is the candidate's inclusive0.4-glyph upper bound, and width133
exceeds the required120. Full-glyph geometry fixes `glyph_h=40`; the component
does not enter the glyph-height estimator or qualify as its own support.
The full-height projection prevents the pre-existing short-row exemption.

| Saved case | Glyph left | Centre difference | Horizontal gap / limit | Component after first filter |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| supported |430|0|47 /60|266 of266|
| too-far |450|0|67 /60|0 of266|

The glyph at430 satisfies both support conditions. The glyph at450 fails
horizontal support, although both cases have matching centre y108. Upper-row
glyphs have centre60, outside the allowed centre distance30, and cannot
provide vertical-only support for this sparse class. The static geometry
therefore agrees with the saved positive-retention and negative-removal results.

Both final band lists remain `[[40,127],[180,219],[340,379]]`, as asserted at
probe line58. This equality is explicitly **not** used to establish retention.
The component-mask intersection with the captured counting copy establishes
the distinction that affected1's final-band-only control could not observe.
A regression removing all sparse components would now fail the positive266
assertion; ignoring horizontal distance would fail the negative0 assertion.
No mutation experiment was executed by this reviewer.

